    "q-108759": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108759",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following do **not** exist?\n\nI. A convex polyhedron with $16$ vertices, $24$ edges, $12$ faces\n\nII. A convex polyhedron with $8$ vertices, $12$ edges, $6$ faces\n\nIII. A convex polyhedron with $24$ vertices, $36$ edges, $14$ faces",
      "worked_solution": "Euler's formula for polyhedra tells us that for any convex polyhedron, we have\n\n$$\nV-E+F=2,\n$$\n\nwhere $V$ is the number of vertices, $E$ is the number of edges, and $F$ is the number of faces of the polyhedron.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given information.\n\n- Polyhedron I cannot exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =16-24+12 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Polyhedron II does exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =8-12+6 \\\\  & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Polyhedron III does exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =24-36+14 \\\\  & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
